CSS **Grid** is the modern two-dimensional layout system — it arranges items in rows *and* columns at once, perfect for page-level layout.

### Define a grid
```css
.container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 200px 200px 200px;  /* three fixed columns */
  gap: 16px;
}
```

### The fr unit — flexible fractions
```css
.container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr 1fr;  /* side columns half the middle */
}
```
The `fr` unit divides remaining space by ratio — much more flexible than pixels.

### repeat() and auto-fit
```css
.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));
}
```
`repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr))` is the magic responsive pattern — it makes as many 200px+ columns as fit, and wraps automatically.

### Rows and area placement
```css
.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
  grid-template-rows: auto auto;
}
.item { grid-column: 1 / 3; }   /* spans both columns */
```

### Alignment
```css
.grid {
  place-items: center;   /* centers items in their cells */
}
```

### Grid vs Flexbox
- **Flexbox**: one direction at a time (row *or* column) — great for components
- **Grid**: both directions — great for page layout

Use them together: Grid for the overall page, Flexbox inside each cell.
